    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_3addeccad247688162798e111f607acf20aa75ed8d0fb8f6b6123a9f5b3460d5",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source gives two linked ways to turn central multiplicity and family structure into leverage. Goldfeld's idea, realized with Gross-Zagier triple-zero examples, converts extra central vanishing into an effective class-number bound. Iwaniec instead engineers a family with forced double vanishing, combines its coefficients to approximate the Möbius function, and moves the remaining burden to family moments and uniform cancellation estimates. The finite-field family analogy and Conrey's conclusion motivate arithmetic geometry as a source of such structure, but do not complete the number-field program.",
